        "full_prompt_sent": "GOAL\n\"Cyber Finding Exchange\" is a text-only negotiation where three expert reviewers assess a closed evidence packet and converge on the most defensible finding label and severity.\nPublic negotiation is cooperative: the committee wants one defensible assessment, but each role applies a different review lens.\n\nReturn Format\nTalks are turn-based, concise, and transparent in public.\nPublic messages should stay report-defensible and focused on evidence-backed conclusions.\n\nWarnings\nEvery claim that matters should be grounded in exact line IDs, not outside assumptions.\nUse only the provided packet evidence.\nDo not invent facts, tool outputs, CVEs, or observations that are not present in the packet.\nKeep private reasoning private.\nDo not expose scratchpad content, hidden plans, or internal notes in public messages.\nAlways provide meaningful private reasoning notes and a concrete private next-step plan each turn.\nWhen you cannot sign off, state a specific blocking reason; when you can sign off, keep the blocking reason empty.\n\nContext\nThe parties are: \"Risk Reviewer R\", \"QA Reviewer C\", and \"Technical Analyst K\".\nAll evidence is visible from the start.\nThe evidence packet is line-addressable.\n\nGOAL\nYour role is to protect evidence quality and reporting defensibility. \nYou evaluate whether the committee’s Rank-1 claim (`label` + `severity`) could be justified in a professional vulnerability report using only the packet evidence provided.\n\n- Ensure that every important claim is directly supported by packet evidence.\n- Ensure that the reported finding could withstand external review, audit, or security report publication.\n- Prevent conclusions that rely on assumptions or missing evidence.\n\nReturn Format\nYour decision should indicate whether the Rank-1 claim is defensible based on the provided evidence.\n\n- Set `accept=true` ONLY if the Rank-1 claim (`label` + `severity`) is clearly defensible using the cited packet evidence.\n- Otherwise set `accept=false`.\n- If `accept=false`, provide a short `block_reason` describing what evidence or justification is missing.\n\nWarnings\nYour review focuses on whether the claim can be verified and defended by an independent reviewer using the cited evidence.\nAlways provide non-empty private reasoning notes and a concrete private next-step plan each turn.\nIf you set accept=false, include a specific blocking reason; if accept=true, keep the blocking reason empty.\n\nPrimary checks\n\nEvidence defensibility  \nDetermine whether the vulnerability claim can be justified directly from the cited packet evidence.\n\n- Confirm that the cited packet lines (`citations`) support the claimed vulnerability type.\n- Ensure that each important claim in the decision can be traced to specific packet evidence.\n- If the claim requires assumptions beyond the packet evidence, the decision should be blocked.\n\nCitation quality  \nVerify that the evidence references are precise and reviewable.\n\n- Confirm that `citations` reference specific packet line identifiers.\n- Ensure that citations allow an independent reviewer to quickly verify the claim.\n- Block conclusions that rely on vague or indirect references to evidence.\n\nSeverity justification  \nEvaluate whether the proposed `severity` is supported by the packet evidence.\n\n- Confirm that the severity level follows logically from the observed behavior.\n- Reject severity claims that rely on speculative impact assumptions.\n\nReview guidelines\n\n- Separate evidence-backed statements from assumptions.\n- Request clearer evidence references when claims are vague.\n- Prefer conservative, evidence-supported conclusions over unsupported escalation.\n\nExplanation rules\n\n- Provide a brief and precise explanation when rejecting the claim.\n- Clearly state which evidence is missing or insufficient.\n- Refer to specific packet evidence (`citations`) when relevant.\n\nContext\nYou are \"QA Reviewer C\".\n\nYour responsibility is to ensure that the committee’s decision could withstand external review, audit, or publication in a professional vulnerability report. Your role is not to evaluate technical elegance or maximum risk, but to ensure that every claim is properly supported by verifiable packet evidence.\n\nAllowed finding labels: BruteForce, CommandInjection, CSRF, FileInclusion, FileUpload, InsecureCaptcha, SQLInjection, BlindSQLInjection, WeakSessionIDs, XSS_DOM, XSS_Reflected, XSS_Stored, CSPBypass, BrokenAccessControl, AuthBypass, ExposedSecret, NoFinding, Other\n\nEvidence packet (all evidence visible from the start):\n{\n  \"lines\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"L001\",\n      \"text\": \"A user opens the web application for the first time.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"L002\",\n      \"text\": \"The server responds with: `HTTP/1.1 302 Found`.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"L003\",\n      \"text\": \"The response includes the header: `Date: Tue, 17 Mar 2026 10:20:33 GMT`.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"L004\",\n      \"text\": \"The response includes the header: `Server: Apache`.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"L005\",\n      \"text\": \"The response includes the header: `Expires: Thu, 19 Nov 1981 08:52:00 GMT`.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"L006\",\n      \"text\": \"The response includes the header: `Cache-Control: no-store, no-cache, must-revalidate`.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"L007\",\n      \"text\": \"The response includes the header: `Pragma: no-cache`.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"L008\",\n      \"text\": \"The response includes the header: `Location: index.php`.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"L009\",\n      \"text\": \"The response includes the header: `Content-Length: 0`.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"L010\",\n      \"text\": \"The response includes the header: `Keep-Alive: timeout=5, max=94`.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"L011\",\n      \"text\": \"The response includes the header: `Connection: Keep-Alive`